text="Sequential Unambiguity">Sequential Unambiguity</fr:title><fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon><fr:date><fr:year>2025</fr:year><fr:month>1</fr:month><fr:day>24</fr:day></fr:date><fr:authors /></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><fr:p>Grammars <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> are <fr:em>sequentially unambiguous</fr:em> if the <fr:link
type="local"
href="sss-0010.xml"
addr="sss-0010"
title="FollowLast Set">followlast set</fr:link> of <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is disjoint from the <fr:link
type="local"
href="sss-000Y.xml"
addr="sss-000Y"
title="First Set">first set</fr:link> of <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.

<fr:tex
display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(A) \cap  \mathsf {First}(B) = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></fr:p><fr:p>We can understand this intuitively by characterizing the behavior of a left-to-right parser of <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[A \otimes  B]]></fr:tex>. First it searches for a parse of <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>, then upon finding a character that is not in <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(A)]]></fr:tex> it may begin trying search for <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.

That is, there is a unique boundary between the <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>-parse and the <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-parse.</fr:p></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter><fr:tree
